From 8f5c1c4c1f668a213e01d263e569afe0f06f8749 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Zaugg <jzaugg@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 01:47:56 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] Merkle: match Scala 3's names for classes nested in objects

Scala 3 keeps an object's class in the name of a class nested in it,
`p.O$.A` where Scala 2 has `p.O.A`, and renders a reference to it the
same way: the stored linearization is made of `ref.baseType(bc)`, whose
prefix is the object class's `this`. So under Scala 3 neither a library
ancestor nor an upstream one nested in an object was found, whatever
the previous two changes did: `p.O$.A` matched no class read from a jar
(`p/O$A.class`, named `p.O.A`), and its binary name candidates never
reached `p.O$A`.

LibraryAncestors.canonical names such classes as Scala 2 does. The
binary name candidates start from it, a library ancestor is matched
against the jar's classes by it, and `conflicts` compares the changed
class's own path with the other paths by it. A lookup by the name as
stored still comes first, since Scala 3 keys this subproject's APIs by
it.

The merkle-libjar-override-nested and merkle-x-abstract-ancestor-nested
tests fail under Scala 3 without this change
(ZINC_SCRIPTED_DEFAULT_LABEL=3.x, against integration/scala3-merkle).
merkle-x-trait-nested and merkle-x-trait-instance add a member trait
nested in an object, and one inherited through an object, mixed in
downstream, under both Scala 2 and Scala 3.
